parallel-fast.snippet:
When three-dimensional bodies contain thin features, non-trivial topology, or surfaces derived from scans, volumetric meshing can become the dominant bottleneck in simulation workflows. Instead of representing these complex bodies one by one with meshes generated through manual trial and error, we introduce a neural atlas framework in which geometries are represented by coordinate charts parametrized by neural networks. The resulting algorithm represents geometry as an atlas composed of overlapping coordinate charts, each equipped with a differentiable neural decoder. Governing equations are pulled back to chart-local reference coordinates via the Piola identity, and local solutions are coupled through multiplicative Schwarz iterations on the overlap graph. Because the atlas is constructed independently of the downstream discretization, it can support a variety of solvers (e.g., a mesh-free physics-informed neural network and a conventional finite-element method). Benchmark and verification studies show that the learned atlas preserves the expected finite-element convergence. Additional numerical experiments demonstrate the feasibility of using the neural atlas to solve both forward and inverse boundary value problems.

Keywords

Coordinate charts
;
Schwarz method
;
Physics-informed neural networks
;
Meshfree methods
;
Complex geometry
;
Elastoplasticity

Introduction

The conventional computational-mechanics pipeline proceeds from a CAD model or surface description through volumetric meshing to a PDE solver [1], [2], [3]. When the spatial domain is geometrically complex — containing thin features, strongly curved interiors, non-trivial topology, or surfaces imported from scanning data — meshing itself becomes a substantial engineering task [4]. This paper introduces an alternative approach that uses neural networks to represent complex geometries, including domains that are not homeomorphic to a sphere. This representation is referred to as a neural atlas herein. I

